<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  THE LOOP KIND NO REPOSITORY HAS CLAIMED YET — a kind that decides NOTHING,
  and exists so that *which kind* can be a run argument with more than one
  legal answer.

  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR. `ai_loop.scxml` is a TEMPLATE, and beside it
  stands one document per KIND holding the decisions the template must not
  make. Until this file existed there was exactly ONE kind, which meant the
  driver could hardcode it and no caller could name one — so every run of the
  template, in every tree, ran under the one kind that exists, whoever its
  author was and whatever work it was doing.

  ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND A KIND IS NOT AN INERT LABEL. A kind names the checker that says
  whether a run may re-aim itself, and such a checker reads ONE tree's own
  record. A run of some other tree, judged by a checker pointed at a record it
  is not about, is not a run with a stricter setting — it is a run being told
  *no* by a document that has never heard of it. That is what one hardcoded
  kind cost, and this file is half of what makes the kind nameable instead.

  ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT THIS DOCUMENT DECIDES: NOTHING. Every clause a kind may hold is
  either absent here or declared empty. It is the honest answer for an author
  who has not yet written a kind of their own — such a run inherits the
  template's own values, and it inherits nobody else's standing yesses,
  working rules, checkers or ceilings.

  ⚠⚠ SO A RUN OF THIS KIND IS UNCHECKED, AND SAYS SO. With no checker named,
  nothing classifies what such a run proposes to work on next — the template
  counts those adoptions and its own closing line reports them, which is the
  point of naming this kind rather than falling through to somebody else's.

  ⚠ IT IS NOT A PLACEHOLDER TO BE FILLED IN. An author who wants decisions
  writes their OWN document beside this one and names that kind instead;
  editing this file would put one author's decisions back into the file every
  unclaimed run reads, which is the hazard the split exists to end.

  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  ⛔⛔⛔⛔⛔ WHAT "INHERITS THE TEMPLATE'S OWN VALUES" ACTUALLY COSTS, AND WHERE
  THE LINE IS — register item 1035, which asked this question of this file.

  `ai_loop.scxml` writes, beside several of its `<data>`, "IT IS THE KIND'S TO
  AUTHOR" — and beside one of them, "not a bound a template other repositories
  copy can choose on their behalf". Ten of those clauses are declared nowhere
  here, so what a run of this kind is bounded by is: reference and
  working_rules, max_turns and reflect_every, reaim_max, stall_after_steps,
  reask_max, hold_within_ms, reflect_after_refusals and context_ceiling —
  every one of them from the template. Measured: reaim_max is 1 there, and a
  run of this kind therefore re-aims itself ONCE and then registers what it
  finds, which is a policy nobody in the tree it is working in wrote down.

  ⚠⚠ SO THE INHERITANCE IS A DESIGN AND NOT A DEFAULT, and this paragraph is
  where that is said out loud. A document with no decisions is the honest
  answer for a tree that has written none; what would be dishonest is the
  MIDDLE — a document deciding a few of those ten and taking the rest from the
  template in silence, because an author who decided some never learnt the
  rest were theirs. `LoopKind::over` refuses exactly that, naming the clauses
  left out, and the two ends stay legal: all of them, or none.

  ⚠ THE LIST ABOVE IS NOT MAINTAINED BY HAND. `LoopKind::CLAIMED` holds it
  beside the reader that answers each clause,
  `what_the_template_claims_for_a_kind_is_what_this_builds_door_asks_for` pins
  that table against the template's own comments, and
  `what_the_kind_that_decides_nothing_leaves_to_the_template_is_named` goes red
  the day this file's answer moves either way.

  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  ⚠⚠ WHAT THIS FILE IS NOT. It is not a second topology, for the reason the
  kind beside it gives at length: a KIND is a datamodel, and what separates one
  from another is consents, standing instructions and prompt wording, never the
  order of the states. The state is final on entry and nobody steps it.

  ⚠⚠⚠ THE THREE IDS BELOW ARE DECLARED AND EMPTY RATHER THAN ABSENT, on the
  rule the template is held to by
  `a_template_names_nothing_of_this_repository_and_decides_nothing_for_the_next_one`:
  deleting a declaration is the cheap way to make that gate green and it breaks
  what the gate is for. Empty is a decision anybody can read; absent is a
  question nobody asked.

  ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THIS DOCUMENT ANSWERS NO ERROR OF ITS OWN, and says so here rather
  than leaving a reader to wonder — the reason is the one written out beside
  its sibling: a `<final>`-only document has nowhere a transition could live,
  W3C SCXML 3.12.2 then ignores an unmatched `error.execution`, and the answer
  is therefore the door's. `document::opened` refuses a document whose start-up
  raised an error nobody answered.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       version="1.0"
       name="unclaimed_loop"
       datamodel="ecmascript"
       initial="decided">

  <datamodel>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHICH DIALOGS A RUN OF THIS KIND MAY ANSWER *YES* TO: NONE.

      A clause here would be a standing yes authorising every unclaimed run, in
      every tree, until somebody edited this file — and the people it would
      authorise are people this author has never met. A caller who needs one
      names it on their own run, where it authorises that run and no other.
    -->
    <data id="may_answer" expr="[]"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT A RUN OF THIS KIND IS REDIRECTED TO MID-FLIGHT: NOTHING.

      A standing instruction is written in one author's own language and typed
      into the next author's agent. It is the weakest of the three — the act's
      first key is a refusal, so it can never grant anything — and it is empty
      here because refusing on somebody's behalf is still deciding on their
      behalf.
    -->
    <data id="screen_rules" expr="[]"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHICH BLOCKED TURNS GET A SECOND AGENT'S OPINION: NONE.

      A criterion here is a standing judgement AND a paid model call per
      blocked turn. A run that did not ask for a second agent must not acquire
      one by naming a kind that had not thought about it.
    -->
    <data id="judged_rules" expr="[]"/>
  </datamodel>

  <!--
    Nothing happens here. The datamodel above IS this document — three empty
    lists and no fourth clause — and the driver reads it without ever stepping
    the machine.
  -->
  <final id="decided"/>
</scxml>
